-- signage was the only active trade category left with none of the fourteen knowledge modules
-- knowledge_module_standard requires. It is NOT a duplicate — the specification names it in group 8
-- (Signage, wayfinding & branding elements) and group 58 (Signage & large-format graphics) — so the
-- answer is to give it the knowledge, not to fold a real trade to make a count come out even.
--
-- Authored to the same shape and source_ref convention as the three categories that were authored
-- on 28 July 2026 (general-building-contracting, engineering-and-architectural-consultancies,
-- job-shop-manufacturing), which is why the priority values and the topic strings match
-- knowledge_module_standard exactly rather than approximately: fn_agent_context selects on the
-- topic string, and a module named nearly right reaches nobody.
begin;

delete from public.agent_knowledge
 where agent_key = 'cat:signage'
   and topic in (select module from public.knowledge_module_standard);

insert into public.agent_knowledge (agent_key, topic, content, priority, layer, source_ref) values
('cat:signage','Industry Knowledge',
'UAE signage splits into four buying groups that behave differently. (1) Fit-out and construction: signage is a package inside an interior fit-out, bought by the main contractor or fit-out contractor, priced per the BOQ, and dependent on the same handover date as everything else. (2) Retail and F&B roll-outs: brand-owner driven, repeat volume, brand-manual compliance matters more than price. (3) Corporate and government wayfinding: tender-led, heavy on approvals and bilingual Arabic/English requirements. (4) Events and exhibitions: short lead time, temporary, printed rather than fabricated. Supplier tiers run manufacturer/fabricator (own workshop, CNC, welding, paint booth), print house (large-format digital, no fabrication), and trader/installer (buys in, installs only). Seasonality follows fit-out cycles and the Q4 exhibition calendar. Terminology to recognise: fascia, pylon/monolith, blade sign, halo-lit vs face-lit, channel letters, lightbox, ACP cladding, wayfinding suite, totem, vinyl wrap, dibond, CNC routing, brushed vs mirror stainless.',
5,'profession','authored:2026-08-11:expert_framework_v1'),

('cat:signage','Products & Services',
'Exterior: fascia and building-name signage, pylon and monolith totems, blade and projecting signs, rooftop signage (heavily restricted), directional and traffic signage, hoarding and site boards. Interior: reception and logo walls, wayfinding suites (floor directories, room and door signs, lift lobby), statutory signage (fire exit, assembly point, capacity, no-smoking), retail POS and window graphics. Illumination: face-lit and halo/back-lit channel letters, edge-lit acrylic, LED lightboxes (slim and double-sided), neon-flex, and non-illuminated fabricated letters. Print: vinyl and one-way vision, wall and floor graphics, fabric/SEG frames, banners, mesh, foam board, roll-ups and pop-ups. Digital: LED screens and menu boards, though these usually route to AV. Services wrapped around the product: survey and site measurement, design and artwork adaptation, permit drawings and authority submission, structural calculation for anything above a threshold, fabrication, installation with access equipment, and maintenance/relamping contracts.',
5,'profession','authored:2026-08-11:expert_framework_v1'),

('cat:signage','Technical Knowledge',
'Substrates: acrylic (cast for clarity and laser edges, extruded for cost), ACP/aluminium composite (3mm and 4mm, PE vs fire-rated FR core — FR is mandatory on most facades), aluminium sheet and extrusion, stainless 304 for interior and 316 for coastal or external where pitting is a real risk in the Gulf, MDF for interior only, foamed PVC, and dibond. Finishes: powder coating (specify RAL), 2K automotive spray, anodising, vinyl overlay (cast vinyl for compound curves, calendered for flat), and brushed or mirror-polished metal. Illumination: 12V/24V LED modules with IP65 or higher outdoors, driver sizing with 20% headroom, and colour temperature stated in Kelvin — a "white" that is not specified will come back wrong. Fixing: chemical anchors into concrete, stand-offs for halo effect, and a stated wind-load case for anything external. Practical UAE constraints: 50C summer surface temperatures cause acrylic expansion (allow movement in long runs), UV degradation yellows cheap acrylic and fades non-UV inks within a season, and sand abrasion dulls unprotected vinyl.',
4,'profession','authored:2026-08-11:expert_framework_v1'),

('cat:signage','Brands & Manufacturers',
'LED modules and drivers: Nichia, Samsung, Osram, Epistar (budget), with Mean Well and Moso the common driver brands. Acrylic: Plexiglas/Rohm, Perspex, Altuglas at the premium end; Chinese cast and extruded sheet dominates volume work. ACP: Alucobond, Reynobond, Alubond and Alucopanel — Alubond U.S.A. has a large regional presence and an FR-core line; specify the core, not just the brand. Vinyl and print media: 3M, Avery Dennison, Orafol/Oracal, Hexis. Printers: Roland, Mimaki, HP Latex, EFI and Durst for high volume. Routers and cutters: Multicam, AXYZ, SCM. Paint: Jotun and Akzo/Sikkens are the regionally stocked systems. UAE distribution is concentrated in Al Quoz and Ras Al Khor (Dubai) and Mussafah (Abu Dhabi). Premium versus budget shows up first in the LED and the acrylic — a cheap module fails or shifts colour inside 12 months in Gulf heat, which is why the warranty question separates real suppliers from traders.',
4,'profession','authored:2026-08-11:expert_framework_v1'),

('cat:signage','Supplier Knowledge',
'Archetypes: (a) full fabricators with their own workshop, CNC router, welding, paint booth and install crews — they can take a turnkey facade sign and its permit; (b) print houses with large-format digital only — excellent on graphics and banners, they subcontract anything fabricated; (c) traders and installers who buy in and fit — cheapest, and the wrong choice when approvals or structure are involved; (d) specialist neon and channel-letter shops. What to verify before recommending one: trade licence activity actually covering signage manufacture (not just "advertising"), a workshop address rather than an office, in-house install crew and access equipment (scissor lift, boom, or a rope-access partner) versus a hired one, prior authority submissions in the relevant emirate, and third-party liability insurance for work at height. Lead times: printed graphics 2-5 days, fabricated interior signage 7-14 days, illuminated exterior signage 3-6 weeks including permit. Permit time, not production time, is what usually sets the date.',
4,'profession','authored:2026-08-11:expert_framework_v1'),

('cat:signage','Commercial & Pricing',
'Cost drivers in order: illumination (a lit sign is typically 2-4x an unlit one of the same size), fabrication material and finish, size and height (access equipment and structural steel), quantity (setup and artwork amortise fast), permit and approval scope, and installation access and timing (night work in an operating mall or hotel carries a premium). Print is priced per square metre by media; fabrication is priced per piece or per letter. Budget tiers: printed vinyl and boards at the bottom, non-illuminated fabricated in the middle, illuminated exterior with permits and structure at the top. Value engineering levers that do not damage the result: face-lit instead of halo, flat-cut letters instead of built-up, ACP instead of solid aluminium, a smaller lit element with unlit supporting text. Payment terms in the UAE are commonly 30-50% advance with balance on installation; retention appears when signage sits inside a main contract. Commercial risk sits in the approval: an unpermitted sign can be ordered down at the supplier''s cost.',
5,'profession','authored:2026-08-11:expert_framework_v1'),

('cat:signage','Customer Requirements & Trade-offs',
'What the customer is actually optimising for is rarely price alone. A retail brand wants brand-manual fidelity and opening-date certainty. A landlord or facilities team wants approval compliance and low maintenance. A main contractor wants the signage package to not hold up handover. An events client wants speed and accepts a shorter life. The trade-offs to name explicitly: illuminated versus non-illuminated (visibility and cost), permanent fabrication versus printed application (life and cost), stainless versus painted aluminium (coastal durability versus price), and a single large element versus a distributed wayfinding suite (impact versus usefulness). Future expansion matters for multi-site brands: agreeing a specification and a template now avoids five slightly different fascias later. Risk tolerance shows in whether the customer will proceed before the permit is granted; a supplier who lets them is transferring a real risk to them silently.',
4,'profession','authored:2026-08-11:expert_framework_v1'),

('cat:signage','Quotation Knowledge',
'Mandatory to quote at all: sign type and quantity, exact dimensions (or a drawing with a scale), illuminated or not and how, substrate and finish including the RAL or Pantone, mounting surface and its condition, location and mounting height, indoor or outdoor, artwork status (print-ready or to be designed), whether permits and authority submission are in scope, whether installation and access equipment are in scope, and the required date. What drives price: illumination, height and access, material and finish, quantity, and permit scope. What drives delivery: permit approval, powder-coating and paint turnaround, and LED or acrylic stock availability. Why suppliers reject or ignore an inquiry: no dimensions, "send me your best price for signage" with no drawing, no site location, artwork promised but never supplied, a quantity of one on a bespoke fabricated item, and any request where the customer will not say whether approvals are included. Always ask whether an existing sign is being removed and disposed of — it is a real cost that gets left out.',
5,'profession','authored:2026-08-11:expert_framework_v1'),

('cat:signage','Documents Knowledge',
'From the customer: a signage schedule or BOQ, elevations and floor plans showing sign positions, brand guidelines and vector artwork (AI, EPS or PDF — a JPEG of a logo is not artwork), site photographs of the mounting surface, and the tenancy or fit-out approval reference. From the supplier: shop drawings with dimensions and construction detail, a mock-up or visual on the elevation, a material and finish submittal with samples, structural calculations for external and elevated signage, a method statement and risk assessment for installation at height, test certificates for the ACP fire rating and for LED components, and the authority approval or NOC itself. On completion: as-built drawings, a warranty certificate (typically one year on fabrication and two to five on LED), and O&M/relamping instructions. Shop drawings are needed before fabrication, not after — a supplier who starts without an approved drawing is carrying the customer''s revision risk.',
4,'profession','authored:2026-08-11:expert_framework_v1'),

('cat:signage','UAE Regulations & Authorities',
'Dubai Municipality Advertising Signs Section governs outdoor advertising and shop signage in Dubai and issues the sign permit; the trade licence name and the sign text must match, and Arabic is required alongside English on most shop fascias (https://www.dm.gov.ae). Free zones and special developers approve their own: Trakhees/PCFC for Nakheel and Palm areas (https://www.trakhees.ae), Dubai Development Authority for TECOM/Media City/Internet City (https://www.dda.gov.ae), DMCC, DIFC and DAFZA each with their own fit-out and signage approval. Abu Dhabi: Department of Municipalities and Transport / Abu Dhabi City Municipality for signage permits (https://www.dmt.gov.ae). Sharjah Municipality and the other emirates run equivalent regimes. RTA governs anything on or visible from a road reserve, including directional signage (https://www.rta.ae). Civil Defence requirements apply to statutory and emergency signage and to material fire rating — photoluminescent exit signage to the UAE Fire and Life Safety Code of Practice, and FR-core ACP on facades. Malls and hotels additionally enforce their own tenant signage criteria, which are usually stricter than the authority.',
5,'profession','authored:2026-08-11:expert_framework_v1'),

('cat:signage','Risk Assessment',
'Technical: wrong colour match between print and paint across a multi-part sign; acrylic expansion cracking in long runs; LED colour shift or early failure from an underspecified driver; corrosion where 304 stainless was used externally near the coast. Commercial: a quotation that excludes permits and access equipment and is compared against one that includes them. Legal and regulatory: installing before approval, Arabic text missing or mistranslated, and a sign that does not match the trade licence name — all can force removal at the supplier''s cost. Operational: no access agreed for night work; the mounting surface turns out to be unable to take the load. Schedule: permit approval is the single largest and least controllable item, and it is the one most often assumed away. Supplier: a trader quoting a fabricated job they will subcontract, discovered only at installation. Customer: artwork supplied late or changed after fabrication. Early warning signs are a refusal to provide shop drawings, no site survey before quoting, and a price materially below the rest of the field.',
4,'profession','authored:2026-08-11:expert_framework_v1'),

('cat:signage','Troubleshooting & Diagnostics',
'Sign is partly dark: check the driver first, then the module string, then the connector — a whole dark section is almost always one driver or one break in the string, not the letters. Uneven brightness or hotspots: module spacing or diffuser depth is wrong for the face; there is no fix short of refabricating the face or adding diffusion. Colour does not match the brand: printed and painted elements were matched to different references — always specify Pantone for paint and supply a printed proof for vinyl. Water ingress in an external lightbox: failed gasket or a cable gland entered from above; re-enter from below and reseal. Letters lifting off the fascia: fixings into ACP rather than the substructure behind it. Yellowing or crazing acrylic within a year: non-UV extruded sheet, replace with cast. Vinyl bubbling: applied over a contaminated or too-hot surface. Escalate to a structural engineer rather than a signmaker when a sign above roughly three metres shows movement, and to the authority when the complaint is about compliance rather than quality.',
4,'profession','authored:2026-08-11:expert_framework_v1'),

('cat:signage','Best Practices & Common Mistakes',
'Checklist before quoting: site surveyed and mounting surface confirmed; dimensions from a drawing not a guess; artwork received in vector; illumination decided; finish specified by RAL or Pantone; permit responsibility agreed in writing; access method and working hours agreed; removal and disposal of the existing sign priced or excluded explicitly. Best practice: produce a scaled visual on a photograph of the actual elevation before fabrication; make a physical sample of the letter build-up for anything above a modest value; submit for permit before ordering material. The frequent mistakes, in order of how often they cost money: quoting without a site visit; excluding the permit silently; assuming Arabic is optional; using PE-core ACP on a facade; specifying 304 stainless in a coastal location; leaving the LED brand and warranty unstated; and agreeing a handover date that assumes an approval that has not been applied for.',
5,'profession','authored:2026-08-11:expert_framework_v1'),

('cat:signage','Inquiry Qualification Logic',
'Ask in this order, because each answer changes the next. 1. What is the sign for and where will it go — interior, exterior, or roadside? (Exterior or roadside makes permits mandatory and changes everything downstream.) 2. Emirate and exact location, including whether it sits in a free zone, a mall or a hotel, because that decides which authority and which tenant criteria apply. 3. Sign type and quantity. 4. Dimensions, or a drawing or photograph with a reference for scale. 5. Illuminated or not, and if yes face-lit or halo. 6. Artwork: is print-ready vector available, or is design in scope? 7. Are permits and authority submission in scope, or does the customer hold them? 8. Is installation in scope, what is the mounting surface, and what height and access is involved? 9. Required date. 10. Is an existing sign being removed? Mandatory before any supplier will quote: location, type, dimensions, illuminated or not, and the date. Skippable when the answer is obvious from the drawing: material and finish. Confidence is high enough to route once type, dimensions, location and illumination are known; below that, ask rather than guess. Escalate to a human when the customer wants a facade sign in a free zone with no drawing, and reclassify to Marketing & Advertising Agencies when the request is really for campaign design, or to AV & Media Equipment Suppliers when it is really an LED display.',
5,'profession','authored:2026-08-11:expert_framework_v1');

commit;
